WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:01.374
Lesson five point three.

2
00:00:01.708 --> 00:00:03.710
How a population of drum windows is

3
00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:05.266
compared with real drummers.

4
00:00:09.233 --> 00:00:11.960
Axis one of the battery asks one question:

5
00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:13.608
does it sound like a drummer?

6
00:00:13.949 --> 00:00:15.028
It needs no guitar.

7
00:00:15.369 --> 00:00:17.812
Lesson five point two turned every two-bar

8
00:00:17.812 --> 00:00:19.232
window into nine numbers.

9
00:00:20.393 --> 00:00:22.726
The human side is the reference: two

10
00:00:22.726 --> 00:00:24.726
thousand four hundred and forty-one

11
00:00:24.726 --> 00:00:27.115
windows of rock beats from the Groove MIDI

12
00:00:27.115 --> 00:00:29.059
dataset, played by nine drummers.

13
00:00:29.392 --> 00:00:31.170
The model plays its own windows.

14
00:00:31.615 --> 00:00:33.636
We never match one window against one

15
00:00:33.636 --> 00:00:34.009
window.

16
00:00:34.328 --> 00:00:36.349
We compare the two populations, one

17
00:00:36.349 --> 00:00:38.797
feature at a time: is the model's spread

18
00:00:38.797 --> 00:00:40.605
of values shaped like the humans'?

19
00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:46.552
Take one feature: the total number of hits

20
00:00:46.552 --> 00:00:47.180
in a window.

21
00:00:47.494 --> 00:00:49.692
Sort the human windows into bins two hits

22
00:00:49.692 --> 00:00:51.786
wide, and count how many land in each.

23
00:00:52.241 --> 00:00:54.825
The middle window has thirty-three hits;

24
00:00:54.825 --> 00:00:57.073
eight in ten sit between twenty-two and

25
00:00:57.073 --> 00:00:57.635
forty-six.

26
00:00:57.972 --> 00:01:00.220
The bar at zero is thirty-seven windows

27
00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:01.344
with no hits at all.

28
00:01:01.832 --> 00:01:03.360
A histogram has two problems.

29
00:01:03.676 --> 00:01:05.784
It's jagged, and its shape depends on

30
00:01:05.784 --> 00:01:07.101
where the bin edges fall.

31
00:01:07.417 --> 00:01:09.261
Worse, look at two or three hits.

32
00:01:09.577 --> 00:01:11.896
No human window landed there, so that bin

33
00:01:11.896 --> 00:01:12.739
is exactly zero.

34
00:01:13.055 --> 00:01:14.741
A model window there would look

35
00:01:14.741 --> 00:01:16.849
impossible, and the measure coming up

36
00:01:16.849 --> 00:01:18.166
divides by these numbers.

37
00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:22.616
Smoothing fixes both.

38
00:01:22.910 --> 00:01:24.327
Here are five made-up values.

39
00:01:24.706 --> 00:01:27.196
Put a small bell curve, a kernel, on every

40
00:01:27.196 --> 00:01:27.514
value.

41
00:01:27.832 --> 00:01:30.216
Add them up, and divide by n, the number

42
00:01:30.216 --> 00:01:32.229
of values, so the total area is one.

43
00:01:32.547 --> 00:01:34.878
That's p hat, the kernel density estimate.

44
00:01:35.349 --> 00:01:37.322
The width of each bell, h, is the

45
00:01:37.322 --> 00:01:37.842
bandwidth.

46
00:01:38.153 --> 00:01:40.387
Too narrow, and the curve is as spiky as

47
00:01:40.387 --> 00:01:41.114
the histogram.

48
00:01:41.426 --> 00:01:43.607
Too wide, and everything blurs into one

49
00:01:43.607 --> 00:01:43.867
hump.

50
00:01:44.331 --> 00:01:46.476
Our code leaves the width to scipy's

51
00:01:46.476 --> 00:01:48.967
default, Scott's rule: the sample's

52
00:01:48.967 --> 00:01:51.286
standard deviation times n to the minus

53
00:01:51.286 --> 00:01:51.865
one fifth.

54
00:01:52.213 --> 00:01:54.763
For the human hit counts, ten point eight

55
00:01:54.763 --> 00:01:57.428
five times zero point two one: about two

56
00:01:57.428 --> 00:01:58.414
point three hits.

57
00:01:59.879 --> 00:02:01.908
Here's the real thing: the human

58
00:02:01.908 --> 00:02:04.363
histogram, faded, with its kernel density

59
00:02:04.363 --> 00:02:05.591
estimate drawn over it.

60
00:02:05.912 --> 00:02:08.260
The empty bin is filled in, and the curve

61
00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:10.449
no longer cares where the bin edges were.

62
00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:14.181
In the code, both curves are worked out on

63
00:02:14.181 --> 00:02:16.301
one common grid: two hundred and one

64
00:02:16.301 --> 00:02:18.420
evenly spaced points, from the smallest

65
00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:20.338
value in either sample to the largest.

66
00:02:20.641 --> 00:02:22.912
Each density then gets a floor, ten to the

67
00:02:22.912 --> 00:02:24.981
minus nine, added at every point, so

68
00:02:24.981 --> 00:02:26.444
nothing is ever exactly zero.

69
00:02:26.747 --> 00:02:28.917
Then each is divided by its own area, so

70
00:02:28.917 --> 00:02:30.027
the area is one again.

71
00:02:33.483 --> 00:02:34.596
Start with surprise.

72
00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:37.379
If the humans give a value probability q,

73
00:02:37.379 --> 00:02:39.884
seeing it surprises you by minus log q:

74
00:02:39.884 --> 00:02:41.331
rare values surprise more.

75
00:02:41.665 --> 00:02:44.225
A coin-flip value, q a half, surprises by

76
00:02:44.225 --> 00:02:45.338
zero point six nine.

77
00:02:45.672 --> 00:02:48.177
A value humans almost never play, q ten to

78
00:02:48.177 --> 00:02:50.625
the minus nine, surprises by twenty point

79
00:02:50.625 --> 00:02:50.959
seven.

80
00:02:51.926 --> 00:02:54.623
KL divergence, D K L of p against q,

81
00:02:54.623 --> 00:02:56.794
measures how surprised the human

82
00:02:56.794 --> 00:02:58.834
distribution is by the model's.

83
00:02:59.229 --> 00:03:02.255
p is the model's density curve; q is the

84
00:03:02.255 --> 00:03:02.781
humans'.

85
00:03:03.275 --> 00:03:05.570
The log of p over q is the extra surprise

86
00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:07.919
at one value: how much more the model

87
00:03:07.919 --> 00:03:09.558
expects it than the humans do.

88
00:03:09.886 --> 00:03:12.126
Weight it by p, so it counts where the

89
00:03:12.126 --> 00:03:14.476
model actually plays, and add it up over

90
00:03:14.476 --> 00:03:15.131
every value.

91
00:03:15.459 --> 00:03:17.699
The log is natural, so the unit is the

92
00:03:17.699 --> 00:03:17.918
nat.

93
00:03:18.758 --> 00:03:19.649
A worked number.

94
00:03:19.983 --> 00:03:22.489
Say a feature has only two values, and the

95
00:03:22.489 --> 00:03:23.992
humans split half and half.

96
00:03:24.326 --> 00:03:26.665
A model that always plays the first value

97
00:03:26.665 --> 00:03:28.224
has p equal to one and zero.

98
00:03:28.699 --> 00:03:31.173
KL is one times log of one over a half,

99
00:03:31.173 --> 00:03:33.410
plus nothing from the second value,

100
00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:35.354
because the model never plays it.

101
00:03:35.707 --> 00:03:38.416
That's log two: zero point six nine nats.

102
00:03:38.885 --> 00:03:41.130
Now swap the order: how surprised the

103
00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:42.644
model would be by the humans.

104
00:03:42.957 --> 00:03:45.098
Half the human mass sits where the model

105
00:03:45.098 --> 00:03:47.343
has only the floor, and it comes to nine

106
00:03:47.343 --> 00:03:48.178
point six seven.

107
00:03:48.491 --> 00:03:49.483
KL isn't symmetric.

108
00:03:49.796 --> 00:03:51.832
The battery uses the model against the

109
00:03:51.832 --> 00:03:54.025
humans: harsh on playing what humans

110
00:03:54.025 --> 00:03:56.322
don't, gentle on leaving out what they do.

111
00:03:57.284 --> 00:03:59.403
Now a model that plays a third value the

112
00:03:59.403 --> 00:04:00.333
humans never play.

113
00:04:00.643 --> 00:04:02.866
There, q is only the floor, ten to the

114
00:04:02.866 --> 00:04:03.434
minus nine.

115
00:04:03.744 --> 00:04:05.915
So KL is log of one over ten to the minus

116
00:04:05.915 --> 00:04:07.465
nine: twenty point seven.

117
00:04:07.775 --> 00:04:09.791
Without the floor it would be infinite.

118
00:04:10.101 --> 00:04:12.427
That's what blowing up means: KL has no

119
00:04:12.427 --> 00:04:14.494
ceiling where humans put almost nothing.

120
00:04:14.958 --> 00:04:17.304
The battery's own code gives the same two

121
00:04:17.304 --> 00:04:19.928
numbers: zero point six nine three, and

122
00:04:19.928 --> 00:04:21.213
twenty point seven two.

123
00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:26.274
The second measure is simpler.

124
00:04:26.585 --> 00:04:28.865
Draw both curves, and shade the area that

125
00:04:28.865 --> 00:04:30.108
lies under both at once.

126
00:04:30.419 --> 00:04:32.750
At each point take the smaller of the two,

127
00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:33.579
and add them up.

128
00:04:34.172 --> 00:04:36.656
Identical curves overlap completely: one.

129
00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:38.869
Curves that never touch: zero.

130
00:04:39.193 --> 00:04:41.515
On the two-value example, the minimum of

131
00:04:41.515 --> 00:04:43.837
one and a half, plus the minimum of zero

132
00:04:43.837 --> 00:04:45.618
and a half: zero point five.

133
00:04:45.942 --> 00:04:48.048
On the impossible value, almost zero.

134
00:04:48.591 --> 00:04:49.444
Both say wrong.

135
00:04:49.786 --> 00:04:52.176
But KL screams at the impossible value,

136
00:04:52.176 --> 00:04:54.510
twenty point seven, while overlap just

137
00:04:54.510 --> 00:04:55.420
says no overlap.

138
00:04:55.762 --> 00:04:57.925
That's why the battery always reports

139
00:04:57.925 --> 00:04:58.209
both.

140
00:04:59.117 --> 00:05:00.914
Now slide a model curve away from the

141
00:05:00.914 --> 00:05:01.245
humans.

142
00:05:01.529 --> 00:05:03.894
In the same place: KL almost zero, overlap

143
00:05:03.894 --> 00:05:04.934
zero point nine eight.

144
00:05:05.218 --> 00:05:07.441
One human spread away: KL zero point five

145
00:05:07.441 --> 00:05:09.049
two, overlap zero point six one.

146
00:05:09.333 --> 00:05:11.225
Three away: KL about five on these

147
00:05:11.225 --> 00:05:13.117
samples, overlap zero point one three.

148
00:05:13.401 --> 00:05:15.293
Six away, overlap has nowhere left to

149
00:05:15.293 --> 00:05:17.421
fall, zero point zero zero three, but KL

150
00:05:17.421 --> 00:05:19.313
keeps climbing, to eighteen point six.

151
00:05:22.717 --> 00:05:25.019
The third number asks how varied the model

152
00:05:25.019 --> 00:05:25.180
is.

153
00:05:25.501 --> 00:05:28.071
Spread here is the standard deviation: the

154
00:05:28.071 --> 00:05:30.320
typical distance of a value from the mean.

155
00:05:30.641 --> 00:05:32.890
The spread ratio, rho, is the model's

156
00:05:32.890 --> 00:05:34.175
spread over the humans'.

157
00:05:34.497 --> 00:05:35.835
One means just as varied.

158
00:05:36.296 --> 00:05:38.411
It exists to catch mode collapse: a

159
00:05:38.411 --> 00:05:40.474
generator that keeps producing the same

160
00:05:40.474 --> 00:05:42.640
narrow few outputs instead of the variety

161
00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:43.310
of real data.

162
00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:45.116
The line is zero point three.

163
00:05:45.425 --> 00:05:47.488
The spec's unit test requires a looping

164
00:05:47.488 --> 00:05:49.809
population to score under it, and the year

165
00:05:49.809 --> 00:05:51.666
explainer calls anything below it a

166
00:05:51.666 --> 00:05:52.646
mode-collapse flag.

167
00:05:52.955 --> 00:05:54.967
The code itself only reports the ratio.

168
00:05:55.831 --> 00:05:57.854
Here's a model with a fifth of the human

169
00:05:57.854 --> 00:05:59.730
spread, centred right on the humans.

170
00:06:00.026 --> 00:06:01.902
Overlap falls to zero point three six.

171
00:06:02.198 --> 00:06:04.567
KL is one point one: where the model piles

172
00:06:04.567 --> 00:06:06.689
up, p is five times q, and that's more

173
00:06:06.689 --> 00:06:08.516
than a whole one-spread shift scored.

174
00:06:08.812 --> 00:06:10.589
But KL grows only slowly as a model

175
00:06:10.589 --> 00:06:12.464
narrows, so the spread ratio is the

176
00:06:12.464 --> 00:06:13.995
measure that catches narrowing.

177
00:06:15.445 --> 00:06:17.751
Now the extreme, on the real reference.

178
00:06:18.088 --> 00:06:20.226
Take one real human window, the one

179
00:06:20.226 --> 00:06:22.645
nearest the middle on all nine features,

180
00:06:22.645 --> 00:06:25.007
and copy it two thousand four hundred and

181
00:06:25.007 --> 00:06:25.907
forty-one times.

182
00:06:26.443 --> 00:06:27.577
Spread ratio: zero.

183
00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:29.467
Overlap: zero point one nine.

184
00:06:29.750 --> 00:06:30.884
KL: one point nine.

185
00:06:31.168 --> 00:06:33.010
Far below the twenty point seven of an

186
00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:35.136
impossible value, but twenty-five to sixty

187
00:06:35.136 --> 00:06:37.074
times the level two halves of the humans

188
00:06:37.074 --> 00:06:38.255
score against each other.

189
00:06:38.538 --> 00:06:40.523
With zero spread the code falls back to a

190
00:06:40.523 --> 00:06:42.555
twenty-bin histogram, so this KL depends

191
00:06:42.555 --> 00:06:43.358
on the bin width.

192
00:06:43.641 --> 00:06:45.484
The spread ratio catches the photocopy

193
00:06:45.484 --> 00:06:45.909
outright.

194
00:06:46.335 --> 00:06:47.885
One caution for the report.

195
00:06:48.229 --> 00:06:50.295
The overall ratio averages the nine

196
00:06:50.295 --> 00:06:52.706
spreads in their own units, so the four

197
00:06:52.706 --> 00:06:55.174
hit counts make up ninety-eight percent of

198
00:06:55.174 --> 00:06:55.346
it.

199
00:06:55.690 --> 00:06:58.101
In practice it's a hit-count spread ratio.

200
00:07:01.923 --> 00:07:03.284
So what is a good score?

201
00:07:03.624 --> 00:07:04.758
Not zero divergence.

202
00:07:05.098 --> 00:07:07.308
Even two groups of real drummers don't

203
00:07:07.308 --> 00:07:08.102
match exactly.

204
00:07:08.442 --> 00:07:10.766
The yardstick is how far humans sit from

205
00:07:10.766 --> 00:07:11.503
other humans.

206
00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:14.369
The spec's sanity test takes the windows

207
00:07:14.369 --> 00:07:16.886
of twenty-four Groove files, splits them

208
00:07:16.886 --> 00:07:19.519
into two random halves, and asks only that

209
00:07:19.519 --> 00:07:21.919
the halves overlap above zero point five.

210
00:07:22.413 --> 00:07:24.772
Our own splits are separate, made on the

211
00:07:24.772 --> 00:07:26.472
full reference for this lesson.

212
00:07:26.801 --> 00:07:29.269
Split window by window, the halves overlap

213
00:07:29.269 --> 00:07:30.476
zero point nine seven.

214
00:07:30.805 --> 00:07:33.383
Too easy: windows from one recording land

215
00:07:33.383 --> 00:07:34.151
on both sides.

216
00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:36.948
Split by recording instead, five different

217
00:07:36.948 --> 00:07:39.581
ways: zero point eight eight to zero point

218
00:07:39.581 --> 00:07:41.446
nine two, with KL under a tenth.

219
00:07:41.775 --> 00:07:44.353
And each drummer against the other eight:

220
00:07:44.353 --> 00:07:46.546
zero point six five to zero point eight

221
00:07:46.546 --> 00:07:46.766
six.

222
00:07:47.095 --> 00:07:49.728
That band is the honest target: a model as

223
00:07:49.728 --> 00:07:52.086
close to the humans as one real drummer is

224
00:07:52.086 --> 00:07:52.744
to the rest.

225
00:07:53.171 --> 00:07:55.261
Against that, the photocopy sits at zero

226
00:07:55.261 --> 00:07:55.990
point one nine.

227
00:07:56.424 --> 00:07:58.266
And a warning from real music: one Lakh

228
00:07:58.266 --> 00:07:59.985
song's own drum part, forty-two windows.

229
00:08:00.231 --> 00:08:01.663
Four fifths of its drum events are

230
00:08:01.663 --> 00:08:03.383
percussion the nine voices don't cover,

231
00:08:03.383 --> 00:08:05.306
cabasa, tambourine, maracas, cowbell and

232
00:08:05.306 --> 00:08:06.821
timbale, so the battery drops them.

233
00:08:07.066 --> 00:08:08.704
It sees only the kick, a clap read as

234
00:08:08.704 --> 00:08:10.177
snare, and a closed hat, mostly

235
00:08:10.177 --> 00:08:11.856
alternating ten and twelve hits a window.

236
00:08:12.101 --> 00:08:13.943
Overlap zero point one three, spread ratio

237
00:08:13.943 --> 00:08:15.826
zero point one: under the line, though

238
00:08:15.826 --> 00:08:16.808
it's a real song's part.

239
00:08:17.150 --> 00:08:19.764
Axis one is a population measure: it means

240
00:08:19.764 --> 00:08:22.051
something across many songs, not on one.

241
00:08:22.378 --> 00:08:24.556
And it hasn't yet been measured for the

242
00:08:24.556 --> 00:08:26.897
drummers in the architecture run, or for

243
00:08:26.897 --> 00:08:28.313
the Lakh drums as a whole.

244
00:08:29.998 --> 00:08:31.920
Compare populations, not windows.

245
00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:34.721
KL catches what humans never play, overlap

246
00:08:34.721 --> 00:08:36.753
shows how much of the human range is

247
00:08:36.753 --> 00:08:39.170
covered, and the spread ratio catches the

248
00:08:39.170 --> 00:08:39.719
photocopy.

249
00:08:40.049 --> 00:08:42.191
The target is the human-to-human level.

250
00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:44.717
This lesson sits beside row five of the

251
00:08:44.717 --> 00:08:46.804
year explainer, with its bell-curve

252
00:08:46.804 --> 00:08:47.188
slider.

